s not 8, 5 or 9. There are three kinds:\n  - a **solid** 3×3 block (9 cells),\n  - an **X** (the 4 corners plus the centre, 5 cells),\n  - a **diamond** (the 4 edge-midpoints, 4 cells).\n- **Pipes.** Each pipe is a wire of colour 8. Cells of colour 5 lie on the wire as decorations. Blocks of colour 9 cover parts of the wiring, and the wire continues out the far side of the block. The pipes start right next to a marker (the cell two steps from the marker's centre). They run, turn and cross one another, and end one blank step before the place where a glyph belongs at the other end.\n\n## The evidence\n\n**Pair 2.** Each of the four solid blocks along the top (1, 2, 3, 4) becomes a diamond in place. An X of the same colour appears at the bottom, at the end of that block's pipe. The pipes are tangled: solid 1 at x=4 ends up at x=12, and solid 3 ends up at x=16. So the target is decided by tracing the wire, not by the column.\n\n**Pair 1.** The same thing happens to solid 3 and solid 2. Their pipes cross: 3's X lands at y=11 and 2's X lands at y=3. The X marker 6 has a pipe that leads to the left, and a diamond of 6 appears there. The two 4-markers are an X and a diamond that are already joined by their pipe, so nothing changes for them.\n\n**Pair 3.** The solid blocks behave the same way. The pipes run under the 9-blocks and come out the other side. The X marker 1 at the bottom produces a diamond of 1 at (2,7), at the top end of its pipe.\n\n## The rule\n\nFor each marker, follow its pipe to the far end. The glyph centre at that end is two steps past the last pipe cell.\n\n- If a marker is already sitting there, do nothing.\n- **Solid** marker: turn the source into a diamond and draw an X of its colour at the far end.\n- **X** marker: draw a diamond at the far end.\n- **Diamond** marker: draw an X at the far end.\n\n## Tracing the pipe\n\n1. Walk straight along cells that are 8, 5 or 9.\n2. When the straight path stops, turn onto whichever perpendicular neighbour is still pipe.\n3. When the walk enters a 9-block, jump to the exit port. A port is an 8 or 5 cell touching the block. Prefer the port that continues straight in the same line. If there is none, use the only other port.\n\n## The test\n\nThe test has four solid sources at the top (1, 2, 3, 6) and an X marker 4 at the bottom right. Each solid becomes a diamond, and its X is drawn at the end of its own pipe. The pipes pass under several 9-blocks along the way. The X of 4 at (21,22) gets a diamond at the end of its pipe, unless a marker is already there, in which case it is skipped.\n\n## Emit checkpoints\n\nThe program emits one canvas per resolved marker, then the final canvas.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid).copy()\n    H, W = g.shape\n    PIPE = {8, 5, 9}\n    X = [(0,0),(0,2),(1,1),(2,0),(2,2)]\n    D = [(0,1),(1,0),(1,2),(2,1)]\n    mask = (g != 0) & ~np.isin(g, [5, 8, 9])\n    markers = []\n    for comp in components(mask, diagonal=True):\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = bbox(comp)\n        if y1 - y0 == 2 and x1 - x0 == 2:\n            n = len(comp)\n            kind = 'S' if n == 9 else ('X' if n == 5 else 'D')\n            markers.append((y0 + 1, x0 + 1, int(g[comp[0]]), kind))\n\n    def inb(y, x):\n        return 0 <= y < H and 0 <= x < W\n\n    def ispipe(y, x):\n        return inb(y, x) and g[y, x] in PIPE\n\n    hidden = {}\n    for comp in components(g == 9):\n        ports = []\n        for y, x in comp:\n            for dy, dx in [(-1,0),(1,0),(0,-1),(0,1)]:\n                yy, xx = y + dy, x + dx\n                if inb(yy, xx) and g[yy, xx] in (8, 5):\n                    ports.append((yy, xx, dy, dx))\n        for cell in comp:\n            hidden[cell] = ports\n\n    def trace(cy, cx):\n        for dy, dx in [(-1,0),(1,0),(0,-1),(0,1)]:\n            y, x = cy + 2*dy, cx + 2*dx\n            if inb(y, x) and g[y, x] == 8:\n                d = (dy, dx)\n                steps = 0\n                while steps < 2000:\n                    steps += 1\n                    ny, nx = y + d[0], x + d[1]\n                    if inb(ny, nx) and g[ny, nx] == 9 and (ny, nx) in hidden:\n                        ports = hidden[(ny, nx)]\n                        exits = [p for p in ports if (p[0], p[1]) != (y, x)]\n                        straight = [p for p in exits if (p[2], p[3]) == d\n                                    and (p[0]-y)*d[1] == (p[1]-x)*d[0]]\n                        if len(straight) == 1:\n                            p = straight[0]\n                        elif len(exits) >= 1:\n                            p = exits[0]\n                        else:\n                            break\n                        y, x = p[0], p[1]\n                        d = (p[2], p[3])\n                        continue\n                    if ispipe(ny, nx):\n                        y, x = ny, nx\n                        continue\n                    nxt = None\n                    for p in [(d[1], d[0]), (-d[1], -d[0])]:\n                        if ispipe(y + p[0], x + p[1]):\n                            nxt = p\n                            break\n                    if nxt is None:\n                        break\n                    d = nxt\n                    y, x = y + d[0], x + d[1]\n                return (y + 2*d[0], x + 2*d[1])\n        return None\n\n    def stamp(cy, cx, col, pat):\n        for yy in range(cy-1, cy+2):\n            for xx in range(cx-1, cx+2):\n                if inb(yy, xx):\n                    g[yy, xx] = 0\n        for a, b in pat:\n            if inb(cy-1+a, cx-1+b):\n                g[cy-1+a, cx-1+b] = col\n\n    def has_marker(cy, cx):\n        return any(my == cy and mx == cx for (my, mx, c, k) in markers)\n\n    for (cy, cx, col, kind) in markers:\n        end = trace(cy, cx)\n        if end is None or has_marker(*end):\n            continue\n        ey, ex = end\n        if kind == 'S':\n            stamp(cy, cx, col, D)\n            stamp(ey, ex, col, X)\n            emit(g.copy(), f\"Solid {col} source becomes a diamond; X of {col} drawn at the far end of its pipe.\")\n        elif kind == 'X':\n            stamp(ey, ex, col, D)\n            emit(g.copy(), f\"X marker {col}: diamond of {col} drawn at the other end of its pipe.\")\n        else:\n            stamp(ey, ex, col, X)\n            emit(g.copy(), f\"Diamond {col}: X of {col} drawn at the other end of its pipe.\")\n    emit(g.copy(), \"All pipes resolved.\")\n    return g\n","candidate_tactics":{"legend":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Markers.** Each marker is a 3×3 glyph in a colour that is not 8, 5 or 9."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"They run, turn and cross one another, and end one blank step before the place where a glyph belongs at the other end."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The glyph centre at that end is two steps past the last pipe cell."}],"paths":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"When the straight path stops, turn onto whichever perpendicular neighbour is still pipe."}],"instructions":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Markers.** Each marker is a 3×3 glyph in a colour that is not 8, 5 or 9."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"They run, turn and cross one another, and end one blank step before the place where a glyph belongs at the other end."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The glyph centre at that end is two steps past the last pipe cell."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":true,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":true},"artifact_sha256":"7e2c6097f3252440171320c3ed3d0d7ea9eed76b82d9e5efcbe518efb589aa2a","task_sha256":"4f7c3bb7eebe0cdf84c90c8558e9dbc6d24dbb8d93eb286228846d2d5a699f23","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
